# Team Rosters (Kesh Memory)

Store canonical team membership here so Jira-aware queries do not re-ask for account IDs.

When the user mentions a team name (for example "Platform team tasks", "team status",
"who is on the Platform team"), look up the roster here first.

## Atlassian context

- **Site**: `<your-org>.atlassian.net`
- **Cloud ID**: `<atlassian-cloud-id>`
- **Primary projects**: `<PROJECT-KEYS>`

## Estimation field reference (this Jira instance)

Replace these with the field IDs from your Jira site.

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `customfield_XXXXX` | Story Points |
| `customfield_XXXXX` | Start Date |
| `timeoriginalestimate` / `timeestimate` / `timespent` | Time tracking (seconds) |

---

## Example team

**Lead Engineer**: Name
**Engineering Manager**: Name
**Total members**: N

### Roster

| # | Name | Title | Email | Jira Account ID |
|---|------|-------|-------|-----------------|
| 1 | Example Person | Engineer | person@example.com | `account-id-here` |

### Default JQL snippet

```
assignee in (
  "account-id-here"
) AND statusCategory != Done
ORDER BY assignee, priority DESC, updated DESC
```

### Default fields to fetch

```
["summary", "status", "priority", "parent", "issuetype", "assignee",
 "customfield_XXXXX", "duedate", "updated"]
```

---

## Usage

1. When the user says a team name, use the roster above; do not re-look up account IDs.
2. When the user mentions a single member by first name only, disambiguate against the roster.
3. Adding members: append to the roster table AND to the JQL snippet.
4. Removing members: delete the row and remove the accountId from the JQL snippet.
5. Add more `## Team Name` sections following the same structure.
